name bfm-merge-1
timeout 300                                  # sim-seconds until TIMEOUT (see bfm-basic.fbm on why)

# THE MERGE — the maximum angle problem. Same defender and same defined manoeuvre as bfm-basic.fbm (a
# sustained max-rate left turn around one unreachable waypoint, run ending at its timeout by design),
# same datalink-off radar-only pursuer. The one thing that changes is the entry: the two jets start 3 nm
# apart NOSE TO NOSE and close at some 800 kt of combined speed.
#
# A head-on merge is the WORST angle problem there is: aspect 180 deg, i.e. the pursuer is as far from
# the target's tail as geometry allows, and the whole engagement has to be built from there in the few
# seconds the closure leaves. It is also the case that separates the two halves of the pilot's pursuit
# logic most sharply — lead pursuit here would fly a collision course, so the closure schedule puts the
# pilot in LAG from the first tick and keeps him there through the pass.
#
# Measured outcome: the lock is taken at 1.9 s and NEVER lost (the pilot turns early enough that the pass
# stays inside the antenna's +/-60 deg gimbal), aspect falls 178 -> 6 deg by t=38 s, and the jet is in the
# control position for 274 of 300 s. The lock-LOSS case is deliberately a separate mission
# (bfm-blind.fbm), where the same merge is flown 300 m off track and the pass whips the line of sight
# past the gimbal.

unit viper
  module f16
  team friendly
  spawn 46.67485 6.97278 4000 270.0 380      # 3 nm east of bandit, pointing straight at him
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode acm_hud
  set task bfm

unit bandit
  module f16
  team friendly
  spawn 46.67485 6.90000 4000 90.0 300
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode off
  wp 46.70000 6.90000 4000 300               # the CENTRE of its turn — see bfm-basic.fbm's header
